# scoursh discovery input - rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.6.3
#
# OPTIONAL for every scan type; only `dast` reads it. A crawl-only DAST run
# works with no config/discovery.conf at all - that is the ordinary case,
# never an error - but a client-rendered (SPA) application's real routes and
# XHR/fetch endpoints are invisible to a static crawl (docs/DESIGN.md §7.5).
# This file feeds the crawler an application's REAL API surface from a
# document you already have: an OpenAPI/Swagger document, a GraphQL schema, a
# Postman collection, or a HAR capture of real browser traffic. All four are
# independent and optional - supply only the ones you have.
#
# Copy this file to config/discovery.conf and edit it.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE CAN NEVER DO: name a host. Every key below is either a LOCAL
# FILE PATH or a bare integer/glob; there is no `base-url`, `host`, or
# `extra-host` key anywhere in this schema (contrast config/scope.conf.example,
# which is the ONLY place a target's host is ever named). A URL named INSIDE
# a supplied document - an OpenAPI `servers[].url`, a Postman request URL, a
# HAR entry's `request.url` - is discarded down to its PATH; scoursh rebuilds
# every request on the `base-url` your config/scope.conf already authorised
# for this target. A spec that names a different host, or a HAR captured
# against a CDN or a third-party analytics endpoint, can never widen scope to
# it (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 19; pinned by tests/suites/dast-crawl.sh's
# "a specification's own host is never scanned" cases). This file decides
# WHAT to test on a target you already authorised, never WHICH host to test.
#
# ONE RECORD PER TARGET. `id` MUST name a target your config/scope.conf
# already defines (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md E080).
#
# Every path below is resolved relative to scoursh's OWN INSTALL ROOT - not
# the scan target, not your current working directory - when it is not
# already absolute; the same convention every other config/*.conf path key
# follows. Nothing below is a default and no host or path named here exists:
# every value is a placeholder you replace with your own.

id: example-target
openapi-path: specs/example-target-openapi.json
graphql-schema-path: specs/example-target-schema.graphql
postman-path: specs/example-target.postman_collection.json
har-path: captures/example-target.har
crawl-depth: 3
include-path: /api/**
include-path: /rest/**
exclude-path: /admin/**
notes: Every endpoint and parameter a specification or capture contributes
  lands in the SAME inventory (reports/<run>/inventory/endpoints.json and
  parameters.json) a plain crawl produces, tagged with the source it came
  from - a spec and a crawl of the same target are additive, not either/or.
  See docs/USAGE.md's "config/discovery.conf" section for how to obtain each
  document type, and rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.6.3 for the frozen field list.
